#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327e406f-f432-5db4-a79f-80195c02cc96" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab06347-a361-5e1d-be5c-dba35197e7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1314" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498820} {:answer/id #uuid "407cfaea-b447-5e42-a30b-1d2cd829a0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1093" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498878} {:answer/id #uuid "a63f4b53-b75c-5bc8-8070-14f83b495105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2641" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499094} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ceab23-5225-54d0-8603-cea96aaf2b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1562" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499163} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3a2118-ab8a-5090-8fc2-f6b42b9b4b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ab06347-a361-5e1d-be5c-dba35197e7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1314" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498820} :answer-field/id #uuid "72921358-da9c-5842-82f0-b8015a6f19c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc097a1b-4231-5bd2-b0eb-22f13d9b5569" :question/math-academy-id "q-143501" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ be a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1335\\,$ | $\\,0.1314\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ | $\\,0.1515\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =50,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{4{x}^{4}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =1.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.25,\\frac{0.1}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.25,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =P(Z<\\frac{1.2-1.25}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.12) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.12)=0.1314.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =Φ(-1.12) \\\\  & =0.1314.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327e406f-f432-5db4-a79f-80195c02cc96" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3315a5cb-452e-528e-8eb5-78dbc3e12104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9992" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498846} {:answer/id #uuid "6de957fe-2080-57db-b272-b3b2f6646ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498974} {:answer/id #uuid "77e88625-4c75-5215-8f5a-0c85b4e5a671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498999} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f8b3c7-8fde-5c2f-927e-132d799da466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8976" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499091} {:answer/id #uuid "f7306d02-503e-55d9-b966-b99018652bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3315a5cb-452e-528e-8eb5-78dbc3e12104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9992" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498846} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d5ea7fd-d63e-51a1-9d3e-f96a0fcebecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cad4258a-bb8c-5f12-a390-574af230e1b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-143509" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-3.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ | $\\,0.0006\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ | $\\,0.0008\\,$ |\n| $\\,-3.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0012\\,$ | $\\,0.0011\\,$ | $\\,0.0011\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.8,\\frac{0.03}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.8,0.001).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.7-0.8}{\\sqrt{0.001}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-3.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-3.16) & =1-P(Z<-3.16) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-3.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-3.16)=0.0008.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =1-Φ(-3.16) \\\\  & =1-0.0008 \\\\  & =0.9992.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9722c125-2d26-5aed-ab60-2e2358a17fdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Discrete"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a341be9f-f5cb-56ef-a964-f9c027945b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061273} {:answer/id #uuid "d292bc3a-8208-5bf5-a235-4cb2c48daf77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5418" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061274} {:answer/id #uuid "3c35e814-4cce-58ab-8005-ddcb9cd68208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5286" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061275} {:answer/id #uuid "57ac6be9-5847-5690-bf26-33234c5f0afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5962" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061276} {:answer/id #uuid "77c0132c-9add-590e-b595-23e65cc0a799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4778" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061277}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d3c36985-e946-50fa-acb6-191dd6a15583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061272}] :question/id #uuid "7d145c65-2193-5d13-9b5d-a2fd49beb6b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-143510" :question/problem "| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nLet $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n = 30$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for $P(\\mid X^(―) \\mid > 0.2)$.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-0.7$ | $0.2389$ | $0.2358$ | $0.2327$ |\n| $-0.6$ | $0.2709$ | $0.2676$ | $0.2643$ |\n| $-0.5$ | $0.3050$ | $0.3015$ | $0.2981$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9722c125-2d26-5aed-ab60-2e2358a17fdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Discrete"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287f4c69-8bd4-5569-9333-3f219db98ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1)-Φ(-1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519056} {:answer/id #uuid "38fb3acb-053d-552f-a825-7ddc2d36cb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519079} {:answer/id #uuid "728b0f84-6442-5c3f-96b2-7e64f3c56829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.5)-Φ(-1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519168} {:answer/id #uuid "798138a1-9c96-5b5d-9bc0-6edae93977b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519182} {:answer/id #uuid "f67a96ba-09f5-59df-948a-4c2d70d715d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1)-Φ(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "798138a1-9c96-5b5d-9bc0-6edae93977b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519182} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ef3aa0-5b31-5f1c-8e8c-420a16ef0aa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e9c32a8-c5ef-5dca-8493-0a63cb017fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143512" :question/problem "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{101}$ be a random sample of size $n=101$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $2.5$ and $3.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=101,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.7+4\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+1.4+0.4+0.8 \\\\  & =2.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{2.6}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.8+1.6+6.4)-6.76 \\\\  & =4.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.6,\\frac{4.04}{101}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.6,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3) & ≈P(\\frac{2.5-2.6}{\\sqrt{0.04}}<Z<\\frac{3-2.6}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.5<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3)=Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5).\n$$"}]}
